# Magnetic torque spins a body up — and honestly grows ENERGY — dynamic notebook
#
# A sphere (mass 1, radius 0.5) carries an isotropic magnetic moment
# tensor M = 0.2*I sitting in the world B-field [0, 0.5, 0]. The
# coupling torque tau = (R M R^T)B = 0.2*0.5 = 0.1 about +y is constant,
# so L(t) = 0.1t exactly: after 4 s, L = 0.4, and with sphere inertia
# I = 0.4*1*0.25 = 0.1 the spin is omega = L/I = 4, E = (1/2)*omega*L = 0.8.
# Watch the hud in the scene window: the E readout GROWS 0 -> 0.8 by
# t = 4 — this torque is NOT derived from a potential, so the simulator
# honestly reports the pumped-in energy (the teaching point). The L
# readout climbs along +y in lockstep, 0.1 per second.
#
# Launch:   posim_notebook magnetic_spin_up
#     or:   cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/magnetic_spin_up.posim
# Press Start in the scene window (or type SCENE START here) to run
# and display the simulation.

# The exact numbers of grammar.md §9 Example 6
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.5, magnetic_moment_tensor = [[0.2, 0, 0], [0, 0.2, 0], [0, 0, 0.2]] }
set system.b_field = [0, 0.5, 0]

# Baselines before anything runs: the body is at rest, nothing stored
get obj0.angular_momentum
energy

scene create
# Default playback dt = 0.01 at ~30 ticks/s reaches the t = 4 anchor in
# about 13 wall seconds — keep it. Zoom in (12 -> 4) so the radius-0.5
# sphere is not a speck in the default +/-10-unit framing.
scene zoom 3
